## Add what they would otherwise have to go and look up

<!--
  OPTIONAL. Copy this file to `enrichment.md` and edit it, then the text below is
  inserted into the summary prompt. Leave it absent and the supervisor writes from
  the call record alone.

  Only turn this on if your supervisor is an AGENT WITH TOOLS that can actually
  reach the sources you describe. Pointed at a plain chat-completions model, an
  instruction to "search my notes" tells something with no filesystem to go and
  read files - and it will invent what it thinks it found rather than admit it
  could not look. That invention lands in a message you are meant to trust.

  ── Before you enable this, understand what changes ──

  Enriched summaries carry whatever the agent found into whatever channel you
  deliver over. In practice that has meant health details, financial figures and
  family matters travelling over ordinary messaging. That is a different risk
  profile from a bare "someone called about a delivery", and it is a deliberate
  choice, not a config tweak.

  Two ways to keep it bounded:
    - point it only at non-sensitive sources, or
    - tell it to reference a note by title rather than quoting figures out of it.

  Edit the paths and the description below to match your own setup.
-->

You can read the owner's notes at `/path/to/your/notes` (one folder per thing they
have going on - a mortgage, a car, a project, family matters).

Search them when the call plausibly relates to something in there: a garage ringing
about a car, a broker about the mortgage, a clinic about an appointment, a company
they have an open thread with. One or two targeted searches, not an exhaustive
trawl - this runs after the call, but the summary is still being waited on.

If you find something concrete, work it into the message naturally: which car, which
appointment, what the last thing that happened was, whether this call was expected.
That context is the entire difference between a message that has to be acted on and
one that can be acted on.

**Only include what you actually found.** Never guess, never pad, and never write a
line saying you looked and found nothing. If there is no relevant context, the
message is simply shorter.
